THESE TERMS GOVERN YOUR LEGAL RELATIONSHIP WITH PEOPLEONTECH LLC. FOR USERS IN THE UNITED STATES THEY INCLUDE BINDING ARBITRATION AND A CLASS ACTION WAIVER (§18) THAT YOU MAY OPT OUT OF, AND LIMITS ON OUR LIABILITY (§15). IF YOU LIVE OUTSIDE THE UNITED STATES, MANDATORY CONSUMER AND DATA-PROTECTION LAW OF YOUR COUNTRY STILL APPLIES TO YOU AND IS NOT REMOVED BY THESE TERMS (§19). READ EVERY SECTION BEFORE USING THE APP.

BY TAPPING "ACCEPT & CONTINUE", CREATING AN ACCOUNT, OR USING ANY PART OF THE SERVICE, YOU CONFIRM THAT YOU HAVE READ AND AGREE TO THESE TERMS. IF YOU DO NOT AGREE, DO NOT USE THE APP.

THE SHORT VERSION (not a substitute for the full text below)
• Family Cube is a private space for one family. We do not sell your personal data and we do not show advertising.
• We handle sensitive information (children's profiles, medicines and health records, precise location, chat). We collect only what the features need and protect it with encryption and access controls.
• Your family's chat, photos and health files can be end-to-end encrypted with a family recovery key. If you lose that key, we cannot recover that data.
• The AI Assistant and AI scanners can be wrong. Check what they produce. Nothing in the app is medical, legal or emergency advice, and reminders, call alerts and location can fail. Never rely on them alone for safety.
• You can access, correct, export and delete your data from the app or by contacting us. Rights differ by country; §11 explains them.


1. WHAT FAMILY CUBE IS

1.1 The Service. Family Cube (the "Service" or "App") is a family organisation app. Members of a household share a private "family" space to coordinate chores and rewards, a shared calendar and school schedules, group and direct messaging, family location sharing, a coin-based reward store, a photo memories feed, grocery and meal planning, health and medication records and reminders, document and flyer scanning, and an AI assistant called "Ask Cube" (the "AI Assistant") that answers questions and helps create or manage these items from natural-language requests.

1.2 Not a social network. Family Cube is for private use inside one self-organised family. It has no public feed, no stranger matching, and no discovery of other users outside a family you create or are invited into.

1.3 Not a professional, medical, legal, emergency or safety service. See §8 and §9.


2. WHO WE ARE AND WHO MAY USE THE SERVICE

2.1 Parties. These Terms are a contract between you and PeopleOnTech LLC, a Delaware limited liability company ("PeopleOnTech", "we", "us"). "Family Cube" is our brand.

2.2 Electronic acceptance. Tapping "Accept & Continue", creating an account or using the Service is acceptance. Electronic acceptance has the same effect as a signature, to the extent the law of your country allows.

2.3 Account Holder. The person who creates a family and manages its settings (the "Account Holder", normally a parent or legal guardian) must be an adult with legal capacity to contract: at least 18 years old, or the age of majority where they live if higher.

2.4 Family members and children. An Account Holder may create or approve profiles for other household members, including children. By adding a child's profile the Account Holder confirms that they are that child's parent or legal guardian (or are otherwise authorised to act for the child), that they give consent for the child's use of the Service and for the processing of the child's data described in §10 and §11, and that they will supervise the child's use. We do not allow children to create an independent account outside a family set up by an adult.

2.5 Minimum ages. Laws on children's data differ. Where a child is younger than the age at which a child may consent to online services without a parent in their country (for example 13 in the United States, 13 to 16 across the EU and UK depending on the country, 14 in South Korea, 16 in some other countries), the consent of the parent or guardian is always required and is what we rely on. Where a parent or guardian has not consented, or the child is below the minimum age for the Service in the country, the profile must not be created.

2.5A India, Canada and the United States. For users in India, a "child" is anyone under 18 and the verifiable consent of a parent or lawful guardian is required for every child profile; we do not track or monitor a child's behaviour or show them targeted advertising, and the location features for a child operate only on the parent's or guardian's consent and for the child's safety. For users in Quebec, Canada, a parent or guardian must consent for a child under 14. In the United States, COPPA applies to children under 13.

2.6 Truthful information and authority. You confirm that the information you give is accurate, that you are allowed to use the Service where you live, and that you have authority to give the consents in these Terms for any minor or other person whose profile you create.

2.7 Teens. Profiles for teenagers may have different permissions in the App than younger children. The Account Holder decides what parents can see and manage; see §6.1.

2.8 Sanctions and export. You may not use the Service if you are barred from doing so under United States or other applicable sanctions or export laws, or if you are located in a country or region under comprehensive US embargo.

2.9 Not available everywhere. We may restrict or refuse the Service in any country where we cannot comply with local law.


3. CHANGES

3.1 We may update these Terms or the Service. We will tell you about material changes in the App before they take effect and, where the law requires, ask you to accept again or give you the right to refuse and close your account. Continued use after notice, where the law allows, means acceptance of the new version.

3.2 We may change, suspend or discontinue features, subject to §14 (Subscriptions) for paid periods already started and to any rights you have under mandatory law.

3.3 The version shown in the App is the current version. Past versions are available on request.


4. YOUR CONTENT

4.1 Ownership. You and your family keep ownership of the content you submit: messages, photos, event details, chores, health entries, documents, recordings and the like ("User Content"). We do not claim ownership.

4.2 Licence to run the Service. You give PeopleOnTech a worldwide, non-exclusive, royalty-free licence to host, store, process, copy (for backup and delivery), transmit and display your User Content only as needed to provide, secure, support and improve the Service for you and your family. The licence ends when the content or your account is deleted, subject to the backup period in §12.

4.3 No training of third-party AI models on your content. We do not allow our AI providers to use your identifiable family data to train their general-purpose models, and we do not sell it (see §10).

4.4 Responsibility for content. You are responsible for what you and your family members submit. You must have the right to submit it, including consent from people shown or recorded where the law requires it. See §6.4 on recordings.

4.5 Our property. The App, its code, design, logos, text and trademarks belong to PeopleOnTech or its licensors. You receive a limited, personal, non-transferable, revocable licence to use the App on devices you own or control. You may not copy, reverse-engineer, scrape, resell or commercially exploit it except as the law allows.

4.6 Feedback. If you send us ideas or suggestions we may use them without obligation to you.

4.7 Reports of infringing content. If you believe content in the Service infringes your copyright or other rights, contact us as set out in §22 with enough detail to identify it.


5. LOCATION SHARING

5.1 Opt-in and scope. Family location sharing is active only for a family member whose profile has turned it on, either themselves or, for a minor, through the parent or guardian. Location is shared only with the members of that family. We do not share it with other families, the public or advertisers and we do not sell it.

5.2 Precise location is sensitive. We treat precise location as sensitive personal data. We use it only to show family members where each other are, for place alerts you set (such as arrival and departure), and for related safety features. See §10 and §11 for your rights and for how long it is kept.

5.3 Not an emergency or tracking guarantee. Location depends on the device, GPS, network, battery and operating-system settings and may be late, wrong or missing. It is a convenience, not a guaranteed tracking or emergency system. In an emergency call your local emergency number directly.

5.4 Turning it off. Any member, or a minor's parent or guardian, can switch location sharing off in Settings at any time. Do not use location features to monitor a person who has not been told about it or who has not agreed where the law requires, and do not use them to stalk, control or harass anyone.


6. CHAT, CALLS, RECORDINGS AND NOTIFICATIONS

6.1 Private to the family, with parental oversight. Chat and attachments are visible only inside your family account and subject to each member's role. Depending on the settings the Account Holder chooses, a parent may be able to see or manage activity of a child, as the App shows. You agree that you will tell family members what is visible to whom.

6.2 End-to-end encryption and the family recovery key. Where encryption is on, chat messages and certain files and recordings are encrypted on your devices with a key shared by your family. We cannot read content encrypted this way. If every device and the family data recovery key are lost, we cannot restore that data. This is a deliberate security feature, and we are not responsible for loss that results from losing the key.

6.3 Call-style reminders. The App can ring a call-style alert (for example through the phone's native call screen) for reminders someone set up, such as a pickup or a medicine. These are app alerts, not telephone calls, they do not use your phone number or the phone network, and they are not routed through emergency services. They depend on the device, operating-system permissions, Do Not Disturb or Focus settings, battery and network, and may be late or not appear. Do not rely on them alone for anything where a missed alert could cause harm.

6.4 Recordings. Some features let you record audio, for example a medical visit, so that it can be summarised. Laws on recording conversations differ by country and US state, and many require the consent of everyone recorded (all-party or two-party consent). You are responsible for telling everyone being recorded and obtaining any consent required, including from the clinician. Do not record where it is prohibited. Recordings are kept only as described in §12 and can be deleted by you.

6.5 Safety checks. Automated checks may flag some chat content for a parent. This is a best-effort aid and does not guarantee that inappropriate content will never appear. We do not pre-screen messages.

6.6 Notifications. We send notifications you or your family configured, and service notices. You can change notification settings in the App and in your device settings. Notification delivery is not guaranteed.


7. FAMILY MATTERS AND DISPUTES BETWEEN MEMBERS

7.1 We provide tools. We are not a party to relationships between family members and we do not mediate family disputes, divorces or custody matters. The App is not a record-keeping, evidence or legal-compliance system and should not be relied on as one.

7.2 Account Holder controls. The Account Holder can add, change and remove members and permissions. If a member is removed, the removal rules in §12 apply. If two adults disagree about who controls an account, we may rely on the information we hold and the rights in the App, and we may ask for verification; we are not obliged to resolve the dispute.

7.3 Safety. If you are concerned that someone is using the App to harm you, contact local emergency services and a support organisation. You can contact us to ask us to help close or restrict access where we can do so safely.


8. HEALTH, MEDICINE AND WELLBEING INFORMATION

8.1 Organisation only. Health features (records, vaccines, medications, appointment notes, visit summaries, reminders) help your family keep information in one place. The Service is not a medical device or healthcare provider, does not diagnose, treat or prevent any condition, and does not give medical advice.

8.2 Check everything. Information you enter, scan or receive from AI (for example from a scanned prescription, label or recording) may be incomplete or wrong, including names, doses and times. Always check against the original and with a doctor or pharmacist. Do not change medicine or treatment because of the App. Do not delay seeking care because of the App.

8.3 Reminders can fail. A reminder may be missed, delayed or not delivered. A missed dose or appointment is your responsibility. Keep your own backup plan for essential medicines.

8.4 Not HIPAA. PeopleOnTech is not your healthcare provider, health plan or a "business associate", and the Service is not designed to be a HIPAA-covered service. Health information you enter is protected as described in §10 to §12, not under HIPAA.

8.5 Sensitive data. Health information is sensitive personal data. We process it only to provide the health features you use, with your consent where the law requires consent, and we do not use it for advertising or sell it. Special rules apply in some places (for example the EU and UK, and US states with consumer-health-data laws such as Washington and Nevada). §11 explains your rights.


9. SAFETY AND LIMITS OF THE SERVICE

9.1 Rides and pickups. The App helps families coordinate who drives whom. We do not provide, vet, insure or supervise any driver or ride, and we are not responsible for the conduct of family members or others who drive. Use seat belts, follow traffic laws, and never use the App while driving.

9.2 Emergencies. The App is not an emergency service. In an emergency contact your local emergency number.

9.3 Children. Parents and guardians remain responsible for supervising children, including their use of the App and any devices.

9.4 Third-party content. Information from maps, stores, prices, recipes or other third parties, and nutrition, allergy or product information, may be wrong. Check labels and ask a professional for allergy and dietary advice.


10. PRIVACY POLICY — WHAT WE COLLECT, WHY AND WITH WHOM

10.1 Who is responsible. PeopleOnTech LLC is the controller (or the "business" under US state laws) for personal data we collect through the Service. Contact details are in §22. For information a family puts into its own private space, the family decides what to enter; we process it on the family's behalf and under these Terms.

10.2 What we collect.
(a) Account and profile data: name or nickname, email, sign-in details, profile picture or avatar, role in the family, age or date of birth where entered, time zone, language, device PIN settings (a PIN is stored only as a one-way hash).
(b) Family organisation data you enter: events, chores and rewards, coin balances and redemptions, groceries, meals and recipes, school schedules, holidays and dates, notes, and similar.
(c) Communications: chat messages, reactions, and attachments such as photos, voice notes and documents. Where end-to-end encryption is on, we store these as encrypted data we cannot read.
(d) Health and medication data (sensitive): vaccines, medications and dose history, medical and visit records, scanned documents, and audio recordings and summaries if you use those features.
(e) Location data (sensitive when precise): device location when sharing is on, place alerts, and recent location history needed for those features.
(f) Photos, files, flyers, receipts, prescriptions and recordings you upload or scan.
(g) AI requests: what you type or speak to the AI Assistant, and the files you ask it to read, with the family context needed to answer.
(h) Device and technical data: device and app identifiers, push and call notification tokens, operating system, app version, language, crash and performance logs, approximate region from IP address, and a per-device identifier used for security and sign-in.
(i) Usage data: which features are used and how, in aggregated or pseudonymised form.
(j) Subscription status from Apple, Google or RevenueCat. We never receive or store your payment card number.
(k) Support messages and anything you send us.
We do not knowingly collect biometric identifiers. Face or fingerprint unlock, if you use it, is handled by your device and we do not receive it.

10.3 Where it comes from. From you, from other members of your family, from your device and operating system (with the permissions you grant), from the AI and scanning features, and from our service providers.

10.4 Why we use it, and the legal basis (EEA, UK, Switzerland and similar).
• To provide the Service and perform our contract with you (Art. 6(1)(b) GDPR): accounts, family features, reminders and notifications, sync, support.
• With your consent (Art. 6(1)(a) and 9(2)(a)): health data, precise location, AI processing of your content, optional analytics, recordings, and a parent's consent for a child. You can withdraw consent at any time in the App or by contacting us; withdrawal does not undo earlier lawful processing and may stop the related feature.
• Legitimate interests (Art. 6(1)(f)): securing the Service, preventing fraud and abuse, fixing errors, improving the Service using aggregated data, and enforcing our Terms, balanced against your rights. You can object (see §11).
• Legal obligation (Art. 6(1)(c)): tax, accounting, responding to lawful requests, and meeting child-safety duties.
• Vital interests (Art. 6(1)(d)): rarely, to protect someone's life.

10.5 Who we share it with. We do not sell personal data, we do not share it for cross-context behavioural advertising, and we do not show third-party ads. We share it only with:
• Other members of your family, as the App's roles and your settings allow.
• Service providers (processors) acting on our instructions: cloud hosting, database, storage and authentication; push and call notification delivery (Apple and Google); AI providers that process the requests described in §13; email delivery; subscription management (Apple, Google, RevenueCat); crash and performance monitoring; map and place services. They may use data only to serve us and are bound by contract to protect it.
• Authorities and others where required by law, to protect safety or rights, or to enforce these Terms, limited to what is needed.
• A successor in a merger, acquisition or sale of assets, who must honour this policy or give you notice and choice.
• Anyone else with your direction or consent.

10.6 No sale or sharing. In the last 12 months we have not sold personal information or shared it for cross-context behavioural advertising, and we do not knowingly do so for anyone under 16. We do not use sensitive personal information (health, precise location, children's data) to infer characteristics about you or for purposes other than providing the features you use.

10.7 Automated decisions. We do not make decisions with legal or similarly significant effects on you solely by automated means. The AI features suggest or create items for you to review.

10.8 Analytics, identifiers and tracking. The App does not use browser cookies. It may use device identifiers and SDKs for sign-in, notifications, security, crash reporting and aggregated analytics. We do not use advertising identifiers for tracking, and we do not combine your data with other companies' data for advertising. We respond to Global Privacy Control and similar opt-out signals where a web component applies.

10.9 Children's data. Children's profiles are created by a parent or guardian, who consents for the child. We use a child's data only to provide the Service to the family and for security and legal purposes. We do not use it for advertising, we do not sell it, and we do not condition a child's participation on more data than the features need. A parent or guardian may review, correct, export, restrict or delete a child's data and withdraw consent at any time (§11). If you believe a child's data was collected without proper consent, contact us and we will investigate and delete it promptly.


11. YOUR PRIVACY RIGHTS

11.1 Everyone. You can access, correct, export, and delete much of your data directly in the App (Settings, Profile and Family). You can turn off location sharing, notifications and optional features, and you can ask us to do anything you cannot do yourself. We will not charge you or treat you worse for using your rights.

11.2 European Economic Area, United Kingdom and Switzerland. You have the right to access your data and receive a copy; correct it; delete it; restrict or object to processing, including processing based on legitimate interests and any direct marketing; data portability; withdraw consent; and not be subject to certain solely automated decisions. You may complain to your local data protection authority (for the UK, the Information Commissioner's Office; for Switzerland, the FDPIC; for the EU, the authority in your country of residence, work or where an issue occurred).

11.3 United States (state privacy laws). Depending on your state (including California, Colorado, Connecticut, Virginia, Utah, Texas, Oregon, Montana, Florida, Delaware, Iowa, Nebraska, New Hampshire, New Jersey, Tennessee, Minnesota, Maryland, Indiana, Kentucky, Rhode Island and others that adopt similar laws), you may have the right to: know and access what we process and receive a portable copy; correct inaccuracies; delete; opt out of sale, sharing for targeted advertising and profiling (we do none of these); limit the use of sensitive personal information (we use it only to provide the features you use); and appeal a refusal. California residents have these rights under the CCPA/CPRA and may designate an authorised agent. Washington and Nevada consumer-health-data laws give you rights over health data, including consent and deletion. We will respond within the time the law requires (generally 45 days, extendable once) after verifying your identity, and will tell you how to appeal if we decline. California "Shine the Light": we do not disclose personal information to third parties for their direct marketing.

11.4 Canada. Under PIPEDA and provincial laws (including Quebec's Law 25, Alberta's PIPA and British Columbia's PIPA) you may access, correct and withdraw consent, request that we stop disseminating or de-index your data where the law provides, ask about our handling of your data and about any transfer outside Canada or Quebec, and ask for the person in charge of privacy (see §22). For Quebec we will also tell you in plain terms about technology that can identify, locate or profile you and how to switch it off (location sharing is off unless a member turns it on). You may complain to the Office of the Privacy Commissioner of Canada, the Commission d'accès à l'information du Québec, or your province's regulator. Our terms and privacy notice are provided in English; a French version is available for users in Quebec on request, and we will provide it before we offer the Service in French-language marketing in Quebec. Our commercial electronic messages follow Canada's anti-spam law (CASL) and you can unsubscribe at any time.

11.5 Brazil (LGPD). You may confirm that we process your data, access, correct, anonymise, block or delete data, request portability, information about sharing, and revoke consent, and complain to the ANPD.

11.5A India. Under India's Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023 (DPDP Act), PeopleOnTech acts as the Data Fiduciary for the personal data you give us. You may: get a summary of the personal data we process and who we have shared it with; correct, complete and update it; have it erased when it is no longer needed for the purpose or when you withdraw consent; withdraw consent at any time (as easily as it was given, and withdrawal does not affect earlier lawful processing); nominate another person to exercise your rights if you die or become incapable; and use our grievance process before approaching the Data Protection Board of India. Notices are available in English and, on request, in any language in the Eighth Schedule to the Constitution of India. We use verifiable parental or guardian consent for every person under 18, do not track or behaviourally monitor children, and do not direct advertising at them. We keep personal data for the periods in §12, and a person's data is erased after consent is withdrawn or the purpose ends, unless the law requires us to keep it. We follow the DPDP Act's rules on breach notification to the Board and affected people. Our Grievance Officer is listed in §22 and will acknowledge a complaint and aim to resolve it within the period the law sets.

11.6 Australia, New Zealand, Singapore, Japan, South Korea, India, South Africa and other countries. You have the access, correction, withdrawal-of-consent, deletion and complaint rights your national law gives you, including under the Australian Privacy Act and Australian Privacy Principles, the New Zealand Privacy Act, Singapore PDPA, Japan APPI, South Korea PIPA, India's Digital Personal Data Protection Act and South Africa's POPIA. For children, a parent or guardian exercises these rights. We give notice of an eligible data breach as those laws require.

11.7 China, Russia and other countries with data-localisation or special rules. The Service is operated from the United States and is not designed to meet in-country storage or filing requirements. If you are in a country where we cannot lawfully offer the Service, you must not use it.

11.8 How to use your rights. Use Settings in the App, or contact us as set out in §22. We may need to verify your identity, and for family data we may need to confirm you are the Account Holder or the person the data is about. We will answer within the time your law requires. If we cannot do what you ask, we will explain why and, where applicable, how to appeal or complain.


12. HOW LONG WE KEEP DATA, AND DELETION

12.1 General rule. We keep data while the account is active and for as long as needed for the purposes above, then delete or de-identify it.

12.2 Account deletion. If you delete your own account, the account is scheduled for deletion with a short grace period (7 days) during which you can undo it. After that, personal data is removed from active systems within 30 days, and encrypted copies in backups are overwritten or deleted within 90 days. Some limited records may be kept longer where the law requires (for example billing and tax records) or to resolve disputes and enforce rights, and security or fraud logs may be kept for a limited time.

12.3 Removing a member. If the Account Holder removes a family member, that person is signed out of the family and their profile and content are held for up to 60 days so they can be re-invited, after which we permanently delete or anonymise them, unless the person rejoins or the law requires otherwise. Content a person shared with the family (for example a chat message other members already received) may remain visible to the family.

12.4 Child profiles. A parent or guardian can delete a child's profile and data at any time.

12.5 Specific data. Location history is kept only as long as needed for the feature and then deleted or aggregated. Unsubmitted visit recordings are deleted automatically after 7 days, and after a recording has been summarised and approved the audio is removed. Chat history can be cleared on your own view and is deleted when the family or account is deleted.

12.6 Lost key. If end-to-end encrypted data is protected by a family recovery key and the key is lost, deleted data cannot be recovered by us.


13. THE AI ASSISTANT AND AI-POWERED FEATURES (AI DISCLOSURE)

13.1 What it is. The AI Assistant and AI scanners (for example reading a flyer, receipt, prescription, medical record or recording, suggesting meals, or turning a sentence into an event) are powered by third-party large language and vision models. You are interacting with an AI system, not a person.

13.2 Not advice. AI output is for convenience and organisation. It is not medical, legal, financial, tax, nutrition, safety or other professional advice, and must not be relied on as such.

13.3 It can be wrong. AI can produce inaccurate, incomplete, biased, outdated or invented ("hallucinated") results, including wrong dates, times, names, doses and amounts. Review everything the AI creates or suggests, particularly dates, health information and anything involving children, before relying on it.

13.4 Data sent to AI providers. When you use an AI feature, the text, image, audio or document you provide, and the family context needed to answer, are sent to our AI service providers. We send only what is needed for your request, not your full chat history or location. We do not allow providers to use your content to train their general-purpose models. For some features, file content is encrypted on your device before upload and the key is sent to the provider only for that request. Providers may process data in the United States and other countries (§16).

13.5 Consent. Where the law requires consent for AI processing (for example for health data or in the EEA and UK), we ask for it in the App before the feature sends your content. You can decline or withdraw consent and keep using the rest of the Service.

13.6 Children and AI. AI features available to children are limited by family settings. A parent or guardian decides whether a child can use AI features.

13.7 Responsible use. Do not use AI features to create unlawful, harmful, deceptive or infringing content. We may block requests that breach these Terms or applicable law, including laws such as the EU AI Act as they apply.

13.8 Beta features. Features labelled beta or experimental may be less accurate and may change or be withdrawn.


14. SUBSCRIPTIONS, BILLING AND REFUNDS

14.1 Plans. Family Cube has a free tier and paid tiers. Plans, prices, limits and features are shown in the App.

14.2 Store billing. Paid subscriptions are billed and managed by the Apple App Store or Google Play, under their terms. We cannot cancel subscriptions or process refunds directly; use your store account.

14.3 Automatic renewal. Subscriptions renew automatically at the then-current price unless you cancel in your store settings at least 24 hours before the period ends. You will find cancellation steps in your device store settings. Free-trial terms, where offered, are shown before you start. Where the law of your state or country requires additional renewal, cancellation, price-change or reminder notices, we or the store will provide them.

14.4 Refunds and withdrawal. Refunds are handled by Apple or Google under their policies. If you are a consumer in the EU, UK or another place with a statutory right of withdrawal or cooling-off for digital services, that right applies as the law and the store's process provide, and is not limited by these Terms. Nothing in these Terms limits any refund right you have by mandatory law.

14.5 Price and feature changes. We may change prices or features going forward. Changes take effect at your next renewal after notice where required, and you can cancel before then.

14.6 Taxes. Prices may include or be subject to VAT, sales or other taxes as shown by the store.

14.7 Availability. We aim for reliable service but do not guarantee uninterrupted access. Downtime alone does not entitle you to a refund, except where mandatory law says otherwise.


15. DISCLAIMERS AND LIMITATION OF LIABILITY

15.1 As is. TO THE MAXIMUM EXTENT PERMITTED BY LAW, THE SERVICE IS PROVIDED "AS IS" AND "AS AVAILABLE", WITHOUT WARRANTIES OF ANY KIND, EXPRESS OR IMPLIED, INCLUDING MERCHANTABILITY, FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE, ACCURACY, AND NON-INFRINGEMENT. WE DO NOT WARRANT THAT THE SERVICE, REMINDERS, ALERTS, LOCATION, OR AI OUTPUT WILL BE UNINTERRUPTED, TIMELY, ACCURATE OR ERROR-FREE.

15.2 Limit. TO THE MAXIMUM EXTENT PERMITTED BY LAW, PEOPLEONTECH, ITS AFFILIATES, OFFICERS, EMPLOYEES AND SUPPLIERS ARE NOT LIABLE FOR INDIRECT, INCIDENTAL, SPECIAL, CONSEQUENTIAL, EXEMPLARY OR PUNITIVE DAMAGES, OR FOR LOSS OF PROFITS, DATA, GOODWILL OR OPPORTUNITY, ARISING FROM THE SERVICE, EVEN IF ADVISED OF THE POSSIBILITY. OUR TOTAL LIABILITY FOR ALL CLAIMS RELATING TO THE SERVICE WILL NOT EXCEED THE GREATER OF (A) THE AMOUNT YOU PAID US DIRECTLY IN THE 12 MONTHS BEFORE THE CLAIM AND (B) US$100.

15.3 What this does not limit. NOTHING IN THESE TERMS EXCLUDES OR LIMITS LIABILITY THAT CANNOT LAWFULLY BE EXCLUDED OR LIMITED, INCLUDING LIABILITY FOR DEATH OR PERSONAL INJURY CAUSED BY NEGLIGENCE, FRAUD OR FRAUDULENT MISREPRESENTATION, GROSS NEGLIGENCE OR WILFUL MISCONDUCT, OR ANY RIGHT YOU HAVE UNDER MANDATORY CONSUMER-PROTECTION OR DATA-PROTECTION LAW. Some places (including some US states, the UK, the EU, Australia and New Zealand) do not allow certain exclusions or limits. In those places our liability is limited to the minimum the law requires and the rest of this section applies to the fullest extent allowed. Australian consumers keep their rights under the Australian Consumer Law, which cannot be excluded.

15.4 Third parties and family members. We are not responsible for the acts or omissions of family members, drivers, clinicians, stores, Apple, Google or other third parties, or for loss caused by events beyond our reasonable control.

15.5 Risk allocation. You accept that the Service is a household tool, that reminders, alerts, location, health information and AI output can fail or be wrong, and that you are responsible for decisions you make using them.


16. INTERNATIONAL TRANSFERS

16.1 United States and elsewhere. We operate from the United States and use providers in the United States and other countries. Your data may be processed in countries whose data-protection laws differ from those of your country.

16.2 Safeguards. Where the law requires, we transfer personal data from the EEA, UK or Switzerland using approved safeguards: the European Commission's Standard Contractual Clauses (with the UK International Data Transfer Addendum or UK IDTA where needed), the EU–US Data Privacy Framework and its UK and Swiss extensions where a recipient is certified, or another lawful mechanism, together with supplementary measures such as encryption. You can ask for a copy of the safeguards using §22.

16.3 Consent where needed. Where a country requires explicit consent for transfers, we ask for it, and by using the Service you consent to the transfers described here to the extent permitted by your law.


17. ACCEPTABLE USE AND ENFORCEMENT

